Theorem 5 (Bounded‑Risk Decision – CMDP with Model Mismatch)

Consider a CMDP with cost bounded in [0, C_max] and strict feasibility (Slater constant ζ > 0). The UEC uses T Monte Carlo rollouts from an imperfect world model with bias bound b. It allows an action only if the empirical cumulative cost estimate Ĵ_c ≤ d − β_T − b. With appropriate margin β_T, the true cumulative cost satisfies E[Σ_t γ^t c_t] ≤ d with probability 1−δ.

Proof.
We use a concentration bound for the empirical mean of T i.i.d. trajectories, each with discounted sum bounded by C_max/(1−γ). By Hoeffding, |Ĵ_c − J_model| ≤ ε with prob. 1−δ for ε = C_max sqrt( log(2/δ) / (2T(1−γ)^2) ). The model bias means |J_model − J_true| ≤ b. Hence |Ĵ_c − J_true| ≤ ε + b with high probability. Choose β_T = ε + b. Then enforcing Ĵ_c ≤ d − β_T ensures J_true ≤ d. The strict feasibility ensures that such actions exist with probability approaching 1 as T grows. For N steps, a union bound gives overall confidence 1 − Nδ. ∎

