Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Robust Control Barrier Functions (RCBFs)

We consider a disturbed control‑affine system

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u + w,\qquad x\in\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n,\; u\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m,

where w\in\mathcal{W} is a bounded disturbance with known compact support \mathcal{W}. The functions f and g are locally Lipschitz. A continuously differentiable function h:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R} defines the safe set

\mathcal{C} = \{x\in\mathbb{R}^n \mid h(x)\ge 0\},\qquad 
\partial\mathcal{C} = \{x\mid h(x)=0\},\qquad 
\operatorname{Int}(\mathcal{C}) = \{x\mid h(x)>0\}.

Definition A.1 (Robust Control Barrier Function). The function h is a Robust CBF on an open set \mathcal{D}\supset\mathcal{C} if there exists a class \mathcal{K} function \alpha (strictly increasing, \alpha(0)=0) such that for all x\in\mathcal{D},

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\inf_{w\in\mathcal{W}}\Bigl[ L_f h(x) + L_g h(x)\,u + \frac{\partial h}{\partial x}\!\cdot\! w \Bigr] \;\ge\; -\alpha\bigl(h(x)\bigr). \tag{A.1}

When \mathcal{U} is polyhedral, the condition can be enforced by solving a robust CBF‑QP:

u^* = \underset{u\in\mathcal{U}}{\operatorname{argmin}} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
L_f h(x) + L_g h(x)\,u \;\ge\; -\alpha(h(x)) + \gamma(x),

where \gamma(x)\ge \sup_{w\in\mathcal{W}} \bigl|\frac{\partial h}{\partial x}\!\cdot\! w\bigr|. The margin \gamma(x) absorbs the worst‑case disturbance. If \mathcal{U}=\mathbb{R}^m and L_g h\neq 0, the closed‑form solution is

u^* = u_{\text{nom}} + \max\!\Bigl(0,\,
\frac{-\alpha(h) - \gamma(x) - L_f h - L_g h\,u_{\text{nom}}}{\|L_g h\|^2}\Bigr) (L_g h)^\top .

With this margin, robust forward invariance of \mathcal{C} is guaranteed (see Theorem 1).

Higher‑order RCBFs. If h has relative degree r>1, a sequence of functions is constructed:

\psi_0 = h,\qquad \psi_i = \dot\psi_{i-1} + \alpha_i(\psi_{i-1}),\quad i=1,\dots,r-1,

and the final condition uses \psi_{r-1} in the RCBF inequality (A.1) with an appropriate disturbance margin. This yields nested invariance of the sets \mathcal{C}_i = \{x\mid \psi_i(x)\ge 0\}, ultimately preserving h(x)\ge 0.

