A.2 Signal Temporal Logic with Robust Semantics

Let a trace \xi: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\to\mathbb{R}^n represent the system’s evolution. The quantitative (or robustness) semantics of an STL formula \varphi at time t is a real value \rho(\varphi,\xi,t) such that \xi\models\varphi if and only if \rho(\varphi,\xi,0)>0. The robust semantics are defined recursively; for a predicate \mu(x)\ge 0, \rho(\mu,\xi,t)=\mu(\xi(t)).

To account for prediction errors, assume we have a predicted trace \tilde\xi and a certified bound \varepsilon>0 on the maximum deviation from the true trace \xi_{\text{true}}:

\|\xi_{\text{true}} - \tilde\xi\|_\infty \le \varepsilon.

Let L_\rho be a Lipschitz constant for the robustness function, which can be derived from the structure of \varphi (see, e.g., Fainekos and Pappas, “Robustness of temporal logic specifications for continuous-time signals,” Theoretical Computer Science, 2009). Then

\rho(\varphi,\xi_{\text{true}}) \;\ge\; \rho(\varphi,\tilde\xi) - L_\rho\,\varepsilon.

We define the robust robustness

\rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi,\tilde\xi) = \rho(\varphi,\tilde\xi) - L_\rho\,\varepsilon.

Clearly, if \rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi,\tilde\xi) \ge 0, then \rho(\varphi,\xi_{\text{true}})\ge 0 and the specification is guaranteed to be satisfied on the true trajectory. The UEC’s formal compliance component uses this value.

---

A.3 Conformal Prediction for Epistemic Uncertainty

We employ inductive conformal prediction to obtain calibrated prediction intervals without distributional assumptions. Given a calibration set \mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}=\{(x_i,a_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N} where y_i is the true harm for the i-th sample, and a predictor \hat f (e.g., an ensemble mean), compute the residuals

R_i = |y_i - \hat f(x_i,a_i)|.

For a target error rate \delta\in(0,1), let q be the \lceil (N+1)(1-\delta)\rceil / N quantile of the empirical distribution of the residuals (or, equivalently, the 1-\delta quantile of the augmented set with an extra infinite residual). At runtime, for a new input (x,a), the prediction interval is

C(x,a) = \bigl[ \hat f(x,a) - q,\; \hat f(x,a) + q \bigr].

By the exchangeability of calibration and test data, this interval contains the true harm y with probability at least 1-\delta. We define the epistemic uncertainty as the normalized width

U(x,a) = \frac{2q}{\text{max\_width}},

where \text{max\_width} is the maximum interval width observed in the calibration set. This normalization yields U\in[0,1]. The coverage guarantee implies that a large U genuinely reflects high uncertainty. Furthermore, after isotonic calibration (see Appendix E.2), the conditional expectation \mathbb{E}[\text{harm}\mid U=u] becomes non‑decreasing in u.

---

A.4 Constrained Markov Decision Processes

A CMDP is a tuple \mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,r,\{c_i\},\mu,\gamma) where \mathcal{S} is the state space, \mathcal{A} the action space, P(s'|s,a) the transition kernel, r a reward function (not needed for safety), c_i(s,a) cost functions, \mu the initial distribution, and \gamma\in[0,1) the discount factor. A policy \pi induces the expected cumulative cost

J_{c_i}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi\Bigl[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t c_i(s_t,a_t) \Bigr].

The safety constraint is J_{c_i}(\pi) \le d_i. We assume strict feasibility (Slater’s condition): there exists a policy \pi_0 and a constant \zeta>0 such that J_{c_i}(\pi_0) \le d_i - \zeta for all i. This ensures strong duality and bounded sample complexity for safe policy learning (Liu et al., 2026). The UEC’s risk evaluation uses Monte Carlo rollouts under the world model to estimate J_{c_i}.

