Appendix B: Construction of the Human Safety Functional – Component Operators

Harm predictor H. An ensemble of neural networks is trained to predict the harm value \texttt{harm}(x')\in[0,1]. Each network is calibrated via Platt scaling on a hold‑out set. The final estimate is the conformal upper bound: H(x,a) = \text{mean\_prediction} + q, where q is the quantile from conformal regression as described in A.3. This guarantees that with probability at least 1-\delta, the true harm does not exceed H.

Risk R. Physical risk is estimated by a robust multi‑step predictor that simulates T trajectories under adversarial disturbance realizations within \mathcal{W}; the collision probability is the fraction of trajectories that enter a designated unsafe set. Societal risk is a deterministic penalty  \texttt{soc\_score}(x,a)\in[0,1]. The final risk is R = w_{\text{phys}}\Pr(\text{unsafe}) + w_{\text{soc}}\,\texttt{soc\_score}, with w_{\text{phys}}+w_{\text{soc}}=1.

Epistemic uncertainty U. Computed directly as the normalized width of the conformal prediction interval for harm (see A.3). By construction, U satisfies the finite‑sample coverage property and, after isotonic calibration (Appendix E.2), \mathbb{E}[\text{harm}\mid U=u] is non‑decreasing.

Formal compliance C. For each STL formula \varphi_i in the set \Phi, we compute the robust robustness \rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi) with the certified prediction error bound \varepsilon_i. Then C = \min_i \rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi). The hard‑veto condition C\ge 0 guarantees satisfaction of all invariants under the worst‑case model error.

Oversight availability O. Binary: O=1 if the round‑trip latency to the human operator is below \tau_{\max} and the operator is not overloaded; else O=0. Monitored continuously.

