Appendix C: Full Proofs of Theorems

C.1 Theorem 1 – Robust Safety Invariance

Proof. The RCBF‑QP enforces, for the executed control u^*,

L_f h + L_g h \,u^* \;\ge\; -\alpha(h) + \gamma(x),

with \gamma(x)\ge \sup_{w\in\mathcal{W}} \bigl|\frac{\partial h}{\partial x} w\bigr|. For any disturbance w\in\mathcal{W},

\dot h = L_f h + L_g h\,u^* + \frac{\partial h}{\partial x} w \;\ge\; -\alpha(h) - \gamma(x) + \frac{\partial h}{\partial x} w \;\ge\; -\alpha(h).

Consider the scalar system \dot y = -\alpha(y) with y(0)=h(x(0))\ge 0. By the comparison lemma, h(x(t)) \ge y(t) \ge 0 for all t\ge 0. Hence x(t)\in\mathcal{C} for all t. For higher‑order RCBFs, the same reasoning applied to each \psi_i ensures \psi_{r-1}(x(t))\ge 0 and consequently h(x(t))\ge 0. ∎

---

C.2 Theorem 2 – Runtime Correctness under Model Error

Proof. The UEC allows an action only if C = \min_i \rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi) \ge 0. By definition, \rho_{\text{robust}}(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi) = \rho(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi) - L_\rho\varepsilon_i \ge 0. Since \|\xi_{\text{true}} - \tilde\xi\|_\infty \le \varepsilon_i by the certified error bound, the Lipschitz continuity of the robustness function gives

\rho(\varphi_i,\xi_{\text{true}}) \;\ge\; \rho(\varphi_i,\tilde\xi) - L_\rho\varepsilon_i \;\ge\; 0.

Thus every formula \varphi_i is satisfied on the actual trajectory. The theorem’s hypothesis—the availability of a verified error bound \varepsilon_i—is satisfiable for systems with well‑characterised sensor noise and dynamics; for arbitrary AI action spaces, such certification is not possible and the guarantee reduces to a conditional one (see discussion in Section 8). ∎

---

C.3 Theorem 3 – Stability under Safety‑First Fallback

Proof. When the soft‑constrained QP is feasible, the control simultaneously decreases a control Lyapunov function V (to a bounded relaxation) and satisfies the robust CBF constraint. The analysis of the original CLF‑CBF QP applies; the disturbance margin in the CBF condition guarantees ultimate boundedness of V to a neighbourhood of the origin.

If the QP is infeasible, the robust CBF property (A.1) guarantees the existence of some u\in\mathcal{U} such that

\inf_{w\in\mathcal{W}}\bigl[ L_f h + L_g h\,u + \tfrac{\partial h}{\partial x} w \bigr] \ge -\alpha(h).

The safety‑preserving fallback selects any such u (e.g., by solving a minimal‑norm feasibility QP), thereby ensuring \dot h \ge -\alpha(h) and, by the same comparison argument as in Theorem 1, h(x(t))\ge 0 for all future times. Thus the hard safety invariant is never violated, even when performance objectives cannot be met. ∎

---

C.4 Theorem 4 – Human Oversight with Calibrated Uncertainty

Proof. We repeat the corrected argument from Section 5. Let U be the conformal uncertainty measure, and assume \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u] is non‑decreasing (obtained via isotonic calibration). Define the essential supremum

h^* = \operatorname{ess\,sup}_{u\in[0,1]} \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u],

and suppose h^* > \varepsilon_h, where \varepsilon_h is the harm achieved by the human overseer. Let \eta = (h^* - \varepsilon_h)/2 > 0. By definition of the essential supremum, the set

S_\eta = \{u \mid \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u] > h^* - \eta\}

has positive probability. Monotonicity implies S_\eta is upward‑closed; therefore S_\eta = (\tau_\eta, 1] for some \tau_\eta < 1, and \Pr(U > \tau_\eta) > 0.

For every u > \tau_\eta, \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u] > h^* - \eta = (h^* + \varepsilon_h)/2 > \varepsilon_h. Integrating over the region U > \tau_\eta yields

\mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U > \tau_\eta] > \varepsilon_h. \tag{C.1}

The UEC decision rule for this threshold is:

· U \le \tau_\eta: execute AI action (harm = \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U]),
· U > \tau_\eta and O = 1: defer to human (harm = \varepsilon_h),
· U > \tau_\eta and O = 0: block (harm = 0).

Expected harm under the UEC is

\mathbb{E}[H_{\text{UEC}}] = \int_{0}^{\tau_\eta} \! \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u]\,dP(u) \;+\; \Pr(U>\tau_\eta,\,O=1)\,\varepsilon_h.

The always‑execute baseline has \mathbb{E}[H_{\text{base}}] = \int_0^1 \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U=u]\,dP(u). The difference is

\Delta = \Pr(U>\tau_\eta)\Bigl[ \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U>\tau_\eta] - p_O\,\varepsilon_h \Bigr],

where p_O = \Pr(O=1\mid U>\tau_\eta). Because p_O \le 1, we have p_O\,\varepsilon_h \le \varepsilon_h. Combining this with (C.1) gives

\mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U>\tau_\eta] - p_O\,\varepsilon_h \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid U>\tau_\eta] - \varepsilon_h \;>\; 0.

Since \Pr(U>\tau_\eta) > 0, \Delta > 0. Thus the threshold \tau_U = \tau_\eta strictly reduces expected harm compared with never deferring. Crucially, the argument holds for any human availability probability p_O\in[0,1], removing the earlier requirement that p_O be “sufficiently large”. An operational threshold can be chosen in practice as the smallest uncertainty bin where the isotonically fitted \mathbb{E}[\texttt{harm}\mid\text{bin}] exceeds \varepsilon_h. ∎

---

C.5 Theorem 5 – Bounded‑Risk Decision under Model Mismatch

Proof. We estimate the cumulative cost J_c(\pi) using T independent Monte Carlo rollouts from the imperfect world model, yielding an empirical mean \hat J_c. The model bias is bounded by b: |J_{\text{model}} - J_{\text{true}}| \le b. By Hoeffding’s inequality, for a single cost dimension, with probability at least 1-\delta,

|\hat J_c - J_{\text{model}}| \;\le\; \varepsilon \;=\; C_{\max}\sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2T(1-\gamma)^2}},

where C_{\max} is the maximal per‑step cost. Hence

|\hat J_c - J_{\text{true}}| \;\le\; \varepsilon + b \quad\text{w.p. } 1-\delta.

Set the margin \beta_T = \varepsilon + b. The UEC only allows an action if the constraint is met with this margin: \hat J_c \le d - \beta_T. Then

J_{\text{true}} \;\le\; \hat J_c + \beta_T \;\le\; d.

For a CMDP with K constraints, apply a union bound to obtain confidence 1 - K\delta. Over N decision steps, another union bound gives overall confidence 1 - NK\delta. The strict feasibility condition ensures that safe actions remain available with high probability as T grows. ∎

